          p: ['Knows the war'],
          e: [
            'Karna was secretly Kunti\'s first son, born before her marriage and set afloat in a river. He was raised by a charioteer\'s family and faced insults because of his birth, even though he was a superb archer.',
            'When others mocked him, Duryodhana made him a king, and Karna became his loyal friend. Before the war, Kunti and Krishna told Karna the truth and asked him to join the Pandavas, his brothers. He refused to betray Duryodhana, though he promised Kunti he would not kill any Pandava except Arjuna.',
            'Karna was famous for generosity — he gave away his protective armour and earrings when asked. Readers see him as a tragic hero. Stories like Karna\'s and Ekalavya\'s (a tribal archer denied training) raise questions about birth, status and fairness that people still debate.',
          ],
          c: [['tradition', 'Karna\'s story spans several parvas; the episode of giving away his armour is especially celebrated.'], ['interpretation', 'Many modern readers and writers, such as Shivaji Sawant in the Marathi novel "Mrityunjay", explore Karna\'s perspective and the injustice of caste-based exclusion.']],
          v: 'tragic hero = a great person whose qualities or circumstances lead to downfall; loyalty = staying true to someone; generosity = willingness to give; identity = who someone is',
